<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lago Oil and Transport Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1924–1985</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lago Oil and Transport Company ran a large oil refinery at San Nicolaas in southern Aruba from 1929 to 1985. Owned by Standard Oil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, later Exxon, it processed Venezuelan crude and was for a time among the largest refineries in the world, drawing workers from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Its closure in 1985 cost Aruba much of its income and pushed the island towards tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lago-oil-and-transport-company.jpg" alt="Lago Oil and Transport Company" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem van de Poll · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Lago-olieraffinaderij_in_San_Nicolas_op_Aruba,_Bestanddeelnr_252-8040.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
